---
/**
 * Redesign option — Flows → Journeys → Playbooks case study.
 *
 * Reframes the live /work/beyond-the-task.astro (currently a heavily
 * placeholder-flagged page — 9+ "[ADD: ...]" evidence markers, no real
 * metrics, explicit "to be verified" language on AI Playbooks status) into
 * the brief's product-evolution story. The product decision described here
 * (AI proposes, admin decides) is the one piece of real content in the
 * source page; everything else that was a placeholder there is marked
 * ContentNeeded here too, rather than invented fresh.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CaseHero from "../../components/CaseHero.astro";
import DecisionBlock from "../../components/DecisionBlock.astro";
import ContentNeeded from "../../components/ContentNeeded.astro";
import ConfidentialityNotice from "../../components/ConfidentialityNotice.astro";
import SignalChain from "../../components/SignalChain.astro";
import FlowsMorph from "../../components/FlowsMorph.astro";
import ScreenGallery from "../../components/ScreenGallery.astro";
const J = "/images/work/journeys";
const FL = "/images/work/flows";
---

<Base title="Flows → Journeys → Playbooks | Kishore Sudhamalla">
  <CaseHero
    eyebrow="Case study 02 · Darwinbox"
    title="From Flows to Journeys to Playbooks."
    lede="Rethinking employee experiences for an AI-native HCM platform. A product evolution story rather than a single redesign. Journeys is released and in use by clients. AI Playbooks is concept and prototype work, with current rollout status still to be verified."
    meta={[
      { k: "Role", v: "Senior Manager, UX Design" },
      { k: "Scope", v: "Product direction, Flows → Journeys → Playbooks" },
      { k: "Status", v: "Journeys shipped · Playbooks conceptual" },
      { k: "Platform", v: "Darwinbox HCM" },
    ]}
    video="/images/work/beyond-the-task/hero-illustration"
    poster="/images/work/beyond-the-task/hero-illustration-poster.jpg"
    alt="Flows, Journeys and AI Playbooks shown as one evolving system (conceptual illustration)"
  />

  <ConfidentialityNotice note="Flows and Journeys are released Darwinbox products. The AI Playbooks work is still a concept and is confidential, so its interface, behaviour and prototypes are withheld and it appears here only as conceptual illustration. This case study focuses on product strategy, design decisions and publicly communicable direction." />

  <article class="container-pg r-case">
    <!-- Context -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Context</p>
      <h2>Flows already existed. The opportunity was in the experience around them.</h2>
      <p>
        Darwinbox had Flows: transactional automation that moved a request through steps such as approvals, notifications and data updates. Flows worked but they were isolated. An employee going through an offboarding or a transfer would hit a series of disconnected tasks, each one correct on its own, with nothing connecting them into a single legible experience.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Complexity -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Complexity</p>
      <h2>Why this was hard.</h2>
      <p>
        A guided experience needs to work for two very different people at once. The admin has to configure something flexible enough to cover many scenarios and customer-specific rules. The employee should never see that configuration complexity, only a clear next step. Layer AI on top and the difficulty compounds. The system has to decide when it is confident enough to propose something automatically and the admin has to stay able to trust and override every step of what it proposes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem framing -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Problem framing</p>
      <h2>The question changed twice.</h2>
      <p>
        The first version of the problem was how to make guided experiences useful across many kinds of workflows. That became Journeys: a guided experience for the employee, configurable by the admin and built on top of the existing Flows.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second version of the problem came once Journeys existed. An admin still had to build every Journey from scratch, even though the system already had the employee data, the system events and the patterns that would let it suggest one. The question changed
        from <em>"how can an admin create a better Journey?"</em> to
        <em>"why should the admin have to manually create every Journey?"</em>
        That reframing is what Playbooks responds to.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Key design decisions -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Key design decisions</p>
      <h2>The decision that shaped Playbooks.</h2>

      <div class="r-decisions">
        <DecisionBlock
          number="1"
          problem="Once the system could recognize a signal or moment (a role change, a location transfer, a new joiner) well enough to suggest a Playbook, how much of the resulting experience should the AI control versus the admin?"
          options={[
            "AI assembles and activates the Playbook directly and the admin reviews after the fact.",
            "AI recommends and proposes steps. The admin reviews, edits and approves before anything is assigned.",
          ]}
          decision="AI proposes. The admin remains responsible for reviewing, editing and approving before anything ships to an employee."
          why="An HCM Playbook touches real employee experiences and often compliance-sensitive steps. The admin needs to stay the accountable party. AI should remove the blank-page problem of starting from scratch, not remove the admin's judgment from a decision that affects real people."
          tradeoffs="Slower time to activation than a fully automated path. The UI also has to carry the extra weight of a clear review, edit and approve step rather than a single accept action."
          result="This human-in-the-loop model is the current design direction for Playbooks. Production status beyond the design and prototype stage still needs confirming. See the note below."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- System: the evolution model -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">System</p>
      <h2>The evolution, as a model.</h2>
      <FlowsMorph />

      <p class="r-evo-chain-label">The emerging interaction model for Playbooks:</p>
      <SignalChain />
    </section>

    <!-- Craft -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Craft</p>
      <h2>The work.</h2>
      <p>
        For admins, Journeys is a configuration surface for choosing which steps, guidance and resources make up a given process. For employees it is a single guided view of what to do and why it matters, instead of a disconnected task list. These are the released screens: the five steps an admin works through to configure a Journey, and the view the employee gets.
      </p>
<p class="r-sub-label">Flows: where it started</p>
      <figure class="r-video">
        <video
          autoplay loop muted playsinline preload="metadata"
          poster={`${FL}/flows-recording-poster.jpg`}
          aria-label="A screen recording of the My Flows list, starting a request from a form, grouping flows by category, and opening one flow to see each stage and who has acted"
        >
          <source src={`${FL}/flows-recording.webm`} type="video/webm" />
          <source src={`${FL}/flows-recording.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
        </video>
        <figcaption>
          <strong>My Flows:</strong> every request an employee has started or is involved in, filterable by status and groupable by flow or category. Opening one shows each stage, who it is assigned to and who acted. Each flow is correct on its own, and nothing connects them into one experience.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="r-sub-label">Journeys: what was built on top</p>
            <ScreenGallery
        id="journeys-screens"
        screens={[
          {
            src: `${J}/journeys-list.webp`,
            name: "Journeys",
            alt: "The Journeys list in the admin settings, a table of Journeys with title, category, number of employees, progress, owner, origin and dates, with Templates and Journeys tabs above it",
            caption: "every Journey in one list, with its category, how many employees it reaches, progress and owner.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/templates.webp`,
            name: "Templates",
            alt: "The Templates tab showing a grid of predefined Journey templates by category and task count, with a Create from scratch option at the side",
            caption: "predefined Journeys to start from, grouped by category, or a blank one built from scratch.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/basic-details.webp`,
            name: "Basic details",
            alt: "Step one of the Create Journey flow with fields for title, category, description, event type, trigger event and user assignments, above a Help and Support section",
            caption: "the first of five steps. What the Journey is and when it applies: title, category, event and trigger, who it is assigned to and the help content attached to it.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/journey-initiation.webp`,
            name: "Initiation",
            alt: "Step two of the Create Journey flow showing initiator details with manual or auto trigger and a table of initiators, initiation steps and step content",
            caption: "the second step. Who starts the Journey and how, manual or automatic, and what content each step carries.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/approval-workflow.webp`,
            name: "Approval and workflow",
            alt: "Step three of the Create Journey flow showing an ordered table of approvers (self, L1 manager, HOD) with an initiation step for each, and a workflow selector below",
            caption: "the third step. The order of approvers and the workflow each step runs through.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/notifications-settings.webp`,
            name: "Notifications",
            alt: "Step four of the Create Journey flow showing a table that maps events to recipients, CC recipients and notification templates, with advanced toggles for confidentiality, auto revoke and hiding completed people",
            caption: "the fourth step. Who is told about which event, plus confidentiality, auto-revoke and visibility options.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/summary-preview.webp`,
            name: "Banner and preview",
            alt: "Step five of the Create Journey flow with banner upload and a gallery of banners on the left and a live preview of the employee-facing Journey on the right",
            caption: "the last step. Choose or upload a banner and preview the employee view before it goes out.",
          },
          {
            src: `${J}/journey-preview-employee.webp`,
            name: "Employee view",
            alt: "A Journey preview page for a first-time manager programme showing the overview, a week by week itinerary, reference documents and a help contact",
            caption: "what the employee sees: the Journey overview, an itinerary by stage, reference documents and someone to ask.",
          },
        ]}
      />
    </section>

    <!-- Collaboration -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Collaboration</p>
      <h2>How this got built.</h2>
      <p>
        Led as Senior Manager, UX Design. I worked with product leadership to define the Journeys to Playbooks direction, alongside product and engineering partners responsible for the underlying Flows engine and the AI recommendation layer.
      </p>
      <ContentNeeded note="Team size, specific collaborators and timeline were not present as concrete facts in the source page. Add them if you want this section more specific than the role-level description above." />
    </section>

    <!-- Outcome -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Outcome</p>
      <h2>What shipped and what was explored.</h2>
      <p>
        Journeys is released and in use by clients. AI Playbooks exists as concept and prototype work built around the admin-approval decision above. It is not presented here as a shipped, measured product.
      </p>
      <ContentNeeded note="No usage data, adoption numbers or client feedback for Journeys were present in the source page. Add real evidence here (adoption, client quotes or a status update on the Playbooks rollout) before publishing. Do not state or imply usage numbers without a verified source." />
    </section>

    <!-- Reflection -->
    <section class="r-case-section" data-reveal>
      <p class="r-case-label">Reflection</p>
      <h2>What this work was really about.</h2>
      <p>
        The interesting part of this project was not the UI for Journeys. It was the shift in the question itself, from "how do we help an admin build something" to "why does the admin have to build it at all." That is the same shift AI-native product design keeps forcing: less time designing the configuration screen and more time deciding how much judgment a system can responsibly take on and how it stays answerable to the person who is still accountable for the outcome.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .r-case { padding-block: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) clamp(4rem, 8vw, 6rem); }
  .r-case-section > :is(p, h2, h3, blockquote, ul, ol, .r-case-label, .cn, .r-decisions, .r-sub-label) { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .r-case-section > :global(.fm), .r-case-section > :global(.sc) { max-width: var(--measure-head); }
  .r-case-section { margin-bottom: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem); }
  .r-case-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-3);
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .r-case-section h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.375rem, 2.6vw, 1.875rem); letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .r-case-section p {
    font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2); margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }
  .r-case-section em { color: var(--color-ink); font-style: italic; }

  .r-decisions { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  .r-evo-chain-label { font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }


  .r-sub-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-ink-3); margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem !important;
  }
  .r-video { margin: 0; }
  .r-video video {
    display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 1920 / 1188;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--gal-r, 1.25rem);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .r-video figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.9rem; max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }
  .r-video figcaption strong { color: var(--color-ink); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
